Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:10:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 04/12] TCSocket: send a zeroed ping with a real module letter

ReConnect() sent its keep-alive ping from an uninitialized STCPacket on
the stack. Only codec_in was set, so 375 bytes of old stack memory went
on the wire, and the module byte was random. Send() then used module
slot 0 only because the random byte was usually not a configured
letter.

Zero the packet and set module to the first configured letter. The
wire format does not change: a ping is still one STCPacket with
codec_in == ping.
---
 TCSocket.cpp | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/TCSocket.cpp b/TCSocket.cpp
index 1dab87f..20f6fbc 100644
--- a/TCSocket.cpp
+++ b/TCSocket.cpp
@@ -486,7 +486,10 @@ void CTCClient::ReConnect() // and sometimes ping
 	
 	if(secs.count() > 5.0)
 	{
-		STCPacket ping;
+		// Zero the whole packet: the old code sent uninitialized stack
+		// bytes, and a random module byte that Send() then had to guess at.
+		STCPacket ping{};
+		ping.module = m_Modules.empty() ? ' ' : m_Modules[0];
 		ping.codec_in = ECodecType::ping;
 		Send(&ping);
 		start = now;
